# Copy to .env, next to this file. `main.ts` reads it through
# `src/config/local-env.ts`; a variable already exported in the shell wins over
# the file, so `CENTRAL_API_SERVICE_TOKEN=… pnpm dev` still does what you expect.
#
# Two of these are required and one is not, and the difference is worth knowing
# before the first boot fails:
#
#   CENTRAL_API_SERVICE_TOKEN  absent is survivable. The service starts,
#     /health answers, and every /api/v1 route answers 503 naming the variable —
#     see ServiceTokenGuard. It is a credential, and a service missing a
#     credential can still be useful.
#
#   CENTRAL_API_WRITE_TOKEN    absent is survivable in exactly the same way, and
#     the failure is narrower: the service starts, reads work, and every write
#     answers 503 naming the variable — see WriteTokenGuard. A second secret
#     rather than a second flag on the first one, because the two are held by
#     different parties: anything allowed to watch the console can be given the
#     read token without being able to change a record.
#
#   CENTRAL_API_DATABASE_URL   absent is NOT survivable, and the boot stops. It
#     is a dependency, not a credential: a service without one would answer
#     /health with `ok` and fail every query, which is a state a load balancer
#     would keep sending traffic to. Better no process than a lying one.
#
# Central API reads only these.
CENTRAL_API_PORT=4000
CENTRAL_API_LOG_LEVEL=info
# PostgreSQL connection string. There is no default and no fallback: a default
# would be a connection string in the repository, pointing every developer's
# console at whichever database the author happened to have.
#
# Before starting the service, apply the migrations and put some rows in:
#
#   pnpm --filter @hewa/central-api db:migrate
#   pnpm --filter @hewa/central-api db:seed
#
# The role and the database have to exist first, and nothing in this repository
# creates them — a migration creates tables, not the database that holds them.
# With a local PostgreSQL already running:
#
#   createdb central_api
#   psql -d postgres -c "CREATE ROLE central_api LOGIN PASSWORD 'central_api'"
#   psql -d postgres -c "ALTER DATABASE central_api OWNER TO central_api"
#
# The credentials below are the ones those two commands produce, which is why
# they are here rather than invented: a connection string in a repository is a
# default, and a default database is whichever one the author happened to have.
# Change them to match whatever you created.
#
# The migrations in `drizzle/` are committed, so nothing needs drizzle-kit to run
# this service — it is a dev dependency of the service that *writes* them.
CENTRAL_API_DATABASE_URL=postgres://central_api:central_api@127.0.0.1:5432/central_api
# The shared secret the Next app presents on every /api/v1 request. There is no
# default: a service that starts with a guessable token is a service anyone who
# read the repository can read. Generate one with
# `openssl rand -hex 32`. The browser never sees this value — the Next server
# reads it and forwards it, which is the entire reason /api/v1 is not called
# from the client.
CENTRAL_API_SERVICE_TOKEN=replace-me-with-32-random-bytes
# The shared secret a caller must present to write, in `x-hewa-write-token`.
# Generate it separately from the one above (`openssl rand -hex 32`) and do not
# reuse the value: a credential that both reads and writes is a read-only
# deployment that can write. A write route requires *both* headers, so this one
# sits alongside the read token rather than replacing it.
CENTRAL_API_WRITE_TOKEN=replace-me-with-another-32-random-bytes
# Comma-separated. Must name every browser origin that calls /health, or the
# browser blocks the response and reports a network error rather than a
# misconfiguration. "*" is accepted for local work and rejected in production.
#
# It applies to /health only. Everything under /api/v1 is served through the
# Next app's own route handlers and carries no CORS headers at all, because a
# browser has no service token to present and a preflight that fails keeps the
# route from being confirmed to exist. See src/config/cors.ts.
#
# This is not access control and is not a substitute for the token above. A
# web app's own origin has to be allowed so the browser can reach it, and
# anything else that reaches central-api must present the token. The CORS list
# exists to keep a browser honest; the token is what keeps a curl honest.
CENTRAL_API_CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3005
